{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed807260302/6/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed807260302/6`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed807260302/6/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\n- `description`：网格中每个彩色条带是一段\"录音\"，将其按特定规则\"回放\"为二维图案：条带中的颜色序列控制画笔在输出画布上的蛇形路径绘制。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。\n  - 网格分为上下两部分，由一条水平 grey 线分隔。\n  - 上半部分包含若干条水平的单像素高彩色条带，每条条带占一行，由多种颜色的像素依次排列组成（无 black 间隔）。不同条带长度可能不同。\n  - 下半部分是一个较大的全 black 空白区域，其宽度等于上半部分中最短条带的长度。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 将每条彩色条带视为一个一维颜色序列。\n  - 所有条带的长度必须是下半部分宽度 W 的整数倍——每条条带的长度恰好是 W 的某个倍数 k，表示该条带编码了 k 行内容。\n  - 将每条条带按宽度 W 折叠为多行：前 W 个像素构成第一行（从左到右），接下来 W 个像素构成第二行（从右到左，即蛇形反转），再下 W 个像素构成第三行（从左到右），以此交替。\n  - 将所有条带折叠后的结果按照条带在上半部分中从上到下的顺序垂直拼接，替换下半部分的 black 区域。\n  - 上半部分（含条带和 grey 分隔线）在输出中移除，仅保留拼接后的画布。\n\n- `Key insight`：第一层推理是发现条带长度与输出画布宽度之间的整除关系，从而确定折叠宽度；第二层是识别蛇形（boustrophedon）折叠规则——奇数行正序、偶数行逆序；两层结合才能正确将一维序列还原为二维图案。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/a1aa0c1e, eval/3e6067c3, train/538b439f, eval/cb2d8a2c, train/48634b99, eval/9385bd28, train/3ad05f52, eval/dbff022c\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a1aa0c1e\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3144\nmechanism=0.4296\nlexical=0.0113\ncode_feature=0.1667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:axis, operation:bfs_distance, operation:count, operation:parity, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/a1aa0c1e.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a1aa0c1e.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCompress the stacked colored bars into a compact ordered table with the control pixel on the selected row.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid contains several full-width colored bars from top to\n  bottom; the last bar is the base/separator color.\n- Each non-base bar may have an aligned three-column comb motif below it,\n  alternating side-pair rows with solid rows.\n- A singleton control pixel sits away from the colored motifs.\n\nRule\n- Make one output row per non-base bar, preserving top-to-bottom order.\n- In that row, paint the bar color once for each solid three-pixel row in\n  its motif, then paint the base-bar color in the separator column.\n- The singleton control pixel sits immediately above the base bar. Among\n  motifs whose open side-pair parity is compatible with that bottom row,\n  put the control pixel in the output row with the fewest solid rows; use\n  horizontal distance to the motif center only to break count ties.\n\nKey insight\n- The vertical comb height is a count, while the control pixel is read by\n  projecting it onto the center gap of compatible comb rows rather than by\n  its vertical band alone.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"full-width colored bars with aligned three-column comb motifs; the final full-width bar is the separator color\",\n  \"dst\": \"compact rows in the same top-to-bottom bar order\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each row with the bar color repeated once per solid motif row, append the separator color column, and place the singleton control pixel on the row whose open motif center is nearest to it\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"top-to-bottom order breaks equal marker-center distances\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"one row per non-separator bar; columns are count slots plus separator and control-pixel columns\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2960\nmechanism=0.3140\nlexical=0.0120\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:path, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/3e6067c3.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/3e6067c3.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"adjacent pixel pair s in a sequence of control pixels in bottom area\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"keep src\",\n  \"dst\": \"merge with adjacent square pair d in an array of squares with colored inner square in main area\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.colors == d.inner_squares.colors\",\n  \"transform\": \"connect d.inner_squares with s[0].color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"process control pairs from left to right\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"control pixels indicates connection path\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"538b439f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2479\nmechanism=0.2727\nlexical=0.0120\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:axis, object:path, operation:count, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/538b439f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/538b439f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel\n  separator line in a non-background color.\n- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.\n- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.\n- Mirror each source object across the separator line.\n- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the\n  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the\n  reflected object in the source color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified\n  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while\n  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"solid rectangular foreground objects all on one side of the full separator row/column\",\n  \"dst\": \"their mirror positions across the separator line\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy each source object by reflection; recolor the rectangular strip between each source object and its mirror to the separator color, overwriting background or distractor pixels inside that strip\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2656\nmechanism=0.3431\nlexical=0.0115\ncode_feature=0.2500\noverlap_tags=code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:path, operation:bfs_distance, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/cb2d8a2c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/cb2d8a2c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Green pixel on grid edge extends toward red bars while maintaining minimum distance from each red bar determined by the count of blue pixels on that bar, then blue pixels turn red.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"green pixel on grid edge s\",\n  \"dst\": \"traverse past all red bars d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"\",\n  \"path\": \"extend from edge toward red bars, turning at right angles to navigate around obstacles\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint green along path, maintaining distance > blue_pixel_count from each red bar\",\n  \"transform\": \"\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: count blue pixels on each red bar to determine minimum distance; Step 2: extend green path from edge maintaining distance from red bars; Step 3: convert all blue pixels to red\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"pathfinding\",\n    \"distance constraint\",\n    \"obstacle avoidance\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\",\n  \"type2\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src2\": \"blue pixels on red bars s\",\n  \"dst2\": \"\",\n  \"match_cond2\": \"\",\n  \"path2\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory2\": \"\",\n  \"transform2\": \"s.color -> red\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"48634b99\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2675\nmechanism=0.3393\nlexical=0.0124\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, operation:count, operation:parity, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/48634b99.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/48634b99.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAdvance the marker from one half of a vertical bar to the same half of the next longer bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms many vertical one-cell-wide bars; the other appears as a single vertical marker segment.\n- The marker segment occupies exactly the top or bottom half of its containing vertical bar.\nRule\n- Recolor the current marker segment back to the bar color.\n- Find the unique all-bar-color vertical bar whose height is two pixels greater than the marked source bar.\n- Recolor the same half of that next longer bar to the marker color.\nKey insight\n- Each bar's total height encodes an ordered size, and the marker advances from a 2k bar to the corresponding 2(k+1) bar while preserving whether it marks the top or bottom half.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"a vertical marker segment occupying the top or bottom half of one bar, plus all-bar-color vertical bars of even heights\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase the current marker to the bar color, then recolor the same half of the unique all-bar-color bar whose height is two pixels greater than the source bar\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2727\nmechanism=0.3473\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
